The NoJIT interpreter reuses its SSA value arrays (FEX 3bec2ac, on top of 0034)

Every interpreted block allocated its own array of SSA values
(IR.GetSSACount() + 1 values of 32 bytes, about 60 KB for a d3d9 block)
and freed it at the end of the block. In NFSU2's menu on an iPad, 64
interpreted blocks (56 in d3d9-emulated.dll) run every frame, which made
about 40,000 rpmalloc allocations and frees of 0xf000 a second, and with
rpmalloc decommitting free pages (before 0033) a VirtualAlloc commit and a
VirtualFree decommit of those pages each time: the main thread's profile
showed aligned_alloc / aligned_free / os_mmap under Interpreter::Run.

A finished run now hands its array back to a small pool (at most 16,
under a mutex), and the next run takes one from there and zeroes it with
assign, which keeps the capacity. A nested run (a syscall that runs guest
code) takes another array or allocates one. The pool itself is allocated
once and never freed.

diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Interpreter/IRInterpreter.cpp b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Interpreter/IRInterpreter.cpp
index 076bbfd..e3248bb 100644
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Interpreter/IRInterpreter.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Interpreter/IRInterpreter.cpp
@@ -615,6 +615,19 @@ bool IsSupported(IROps Op) {
   }
 }
 
+// WinPad: SSA value arrays of finished runs, kept for the next ones. A run used to allocate its own
+// (IR.GetSSACount() + 1 values of 32 bytes, about 60 KB for a typical d3d9 block) and free it at the
+// end, so every interpreted block was an rpmalloc allocation and free, and with rpmalloc's free
+// pages decommitted a commit and decommit of those pages too: about 40,000 a second in NFSU2's
+// menu. A nested run (a syscall that runs guest code) takes another array, or allocates one. The
+// pool is never freed, so no destructor frees into the allocator at process exit.
+std::mutex SpareValsLock;
+fextl::vector<fextl::vector<Val>>& SpareVals() {
+  static auto* Pool = new fextl::vector<fextl::vector<Val>>();
+  return *Pool;
+}
+constexpr size_t MaxSpareVals = 16;
+
 class Interpreter {
 public:
   Interpreter(Context::ContextImpl* CTX, Core::CpuStateFrame* Frame, const InterpreterIR& Copy, uint64_t Entry)
@@ -624,9 +637,29 @@ public:
          Copy.DataSize, Copy.ListSize)
     , Entry {Entry}
     , GuestBase {CTX->GetGuestBase()} {
-    Vals.resize(IR.GetSSACount() + 1);
+    {
+      std::scoped_lock lk(SpareValsLock);
+      auto& Pool = SpareVals();
+      if (!Pool.empty()) {
+        Vals = std::move(Pool.back());
+        Pool.pop_back();
+      }
+    }
+    // Zeroed as resize did on a fresh array; keeps the capacity, so no allocation once it is big enough.
+    Vals.assign(IR.GetSSACount() + 1, Val {});
   }
 
+  ~Interpreter() {
+    std::scoped_lock lk(SpareValsLock);
+    auto& Pool = SpareVals();
+    if (Pool.size() < MaxSpareVals) {
+      Pool.push_back(std::move(Vals));
+    }
+  }
+
+  Interpreter(const Interpreter&) = delete;
+  Interpreter& operator=(const Interpreter&) = delete;
+
   uintptr_t Run();
 
 private:
